# BEGIN LOOM-MANAGED (merge drivers, #4528)
# install-metadata.json is a machine-local install stamp (loom_version,
# loom_commit, last_resync, loom_source) that every host's resync re-writes --
# always keep our side on a merge conflict; the file is fully re-derived by
# the next resync regardless of which side "wins". A `merge=ours` attribute
# alone is inert: the `ours` driver must also be enabled in local (never
# committed) git config -- `git config merge.ours.driver true` -- which Loom's
# install and resync scripts set automatically (see #4528).
#
# Field-level guardrail (#7359): this `merge=ours` driver silently discards
# whichever side loses on ANY field of this file, but only the `loom_version`
# field is actually checked afterward -- by scripts/version.sh's
# check_versions() (invoked as `./scripts/version.sh check`) and the
# version-check-gate.sh CI gate. The other fields (`installed_files`,
# `loom_source_remote`, `install_date`, `last_resync`, etc.) have no such
# gate: a hand-edit to any of them that collides with an upstream touch to
# this file during a rebase/merge would be silently dropped with nothing to
# catch it. This is accepted as-is (see #7359) rather than broadening the
# gate, because every field but `loom_version` is resync-owned and, per the
# note above, "fully re-derived by the next resync regardless of which side
# wins" -- so a dropped hand-edit to those fields is expected to self-heal on
# the next resync. Consequently: never hand-edit `installed_files`,
# `loom_source_remote`, `install_date`, or `last_resync` in a feature commit;
# only defaults/scripts/resync-installed.sh's restamp_metadata() should write
# them.
.loom/install-metadata.json merge=ours
# END LOOM-MANAGED (merge drivers, #4528)
